A||||6||Zack Doyle||Idle and back fire problems||||8||
Z||000000||Zack Doyle||12-13-2001||06:19 PM||||I have been haveing problem with a bad idle.  After the car has warmed up and the idle has settled it has a miss fire and the idle drops then goes back up.  A second later it does it again.  I have a 1988 2.5L. coupe.<P>The other problem is the back fire.  At the last oil change the oil change guys over torqued the oil cap and we had to take it to the dealership to get it off.  There they put 5 quarts of oil instead of 4 and the back fire started.  I changed the oil and put the right amount of oil however the back fire is still there.  I have had to replace the air filter 3 times because oil is damageing the inside of the filter.  Not the outside. * It only happens when the car is gas pedal is being floored.||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||12-13-2001||08:22 PM||||Check for blown off vac lines. check PCV line and clamp all joints. (Gently or you'll ruin the elbows.)<P>replace MAP and/or MAT. <P>both can be damaged by a back fire. MAT can be damaged by carbon buildup. Oil in intake could also carbon it up....<P>both will also agravate backfire, especially when snapped from idle to WOT.<BR>MAP is used heavily in timing calcs. MAT is used for F/A ratio tuning.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||12-14-2001||08:17 AM||||I had a backfire afer washing my motor.  Blew the tip off the oxygen sensor.  Never set the check engine light off, but the car ran bad.  Pulled the ox sensor out and the only thing left on the bottom was the threads that screwed into the manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||12-14-2001||08:29 AM||||Does it have platinum plugs in it? If so replace them. Also check the MAP sensor.<P>Oh and even if you don't have platinum plugs CHECK the gap! DIS does not like big spark gaps.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTDude||12-14-2001||06:05 PM||||Overfilling it with oil may just be a coincidence.  Sounds like a stuck or burnt intake valve to me.  Do a compression test.  That will tell you for sure.  Or you can can remove the valve cover....loosen all the rocker arm bolts....so that all the valves are closed...then put air pressure in each cylinder.  If you hear air coming out of the throttle body, it's the intake valve for that cylinder....air from exhaust....exhaust valve.<P>I hope it's something simpler....<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||12-14-2001||06:11 PM||||I don't have an answer, but how in the hell can someone over torque the oil cap???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>I always change my own oil because of some of the stories I hear. Plus it ain't hard to do at all. Anyway, good luck with your problem.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif"> <BR><B>86 SE V6 - daily driver</B><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 Sport Coupe 2.5L 5spd (soon to be sold)</FONT>||||reg||11
Z||000006||Zack Doyle||12-14-2001||06:53 PM||||I have changed the O2 sensor, done a compression check and dad is still looking for the MAT and MAP sensors.  He found one for the wrong car and I put it in and haven't taken it out.||||reg||8
